Z||000008||Patrick's Dad||07-01-2000||08:08 AM||||Hey, Monk! Welcome to home ownership!<P>We bought "The Happy Brown House" (My three year old son named it) just this past October. It's 112 years old. I can't make a straight wiring run from the attic to the basement....<P>My wife's truck also ran over a small tree in our side yard. I didn't have anything to do with it. I wasn't even in it. (I left it in N instead of P by accident....). Soon, I gotta pull the rest of the tree up.<P>A garden will be nice, too.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||10
Z||000009||batboy||07-01-2000||08:51 AM||||My house is slightly more than a century old too. Nothing fancy, just home, sweet home.<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/HomeBackyard.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000010||Wolfhound||07-01-2000||09:16 AM||||I love old houses. Made to last of old growth wood. How many houses built today will be here a 100+ years from now. I work a lot of new construction and see half million dollar houses that are basicly disposable.<BR> Love the trees Batboy, really sets the house off. Is the coupe a chevy?<BR>Coy <BR> ||||reg||1
Z||000011||Monkeyman||07-01-2000||09:21 AM||||I like the looks of the older houses, but I'd really like a fairly small, bungalow (log cabin).  About 1200 square feet would be plenty for me and the missus.  Our house was built in the late '30s.  There have been a couple small additions so half of the wiring is nice and (fairly) new, but the main part of the house has old fabric covered wiring.  If we stay here very long (my wife's already talking about moving in a couple of years!) I'll probably have an electrician rewire the old part of the house.  The threat of fire makes me nervous.  We just had a privacy fence installed (<B>$$$</B>) but still need to stain it.  We're getting our dog up here today so hopefully he won't get in the way while we throw the stain on it.  I'm trying to get the garage "livable" but there are tons of spider webs (and some spiders, unfortunately <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">) currently residing between the studs.  I plan to put some masonite on the walls.  Any suggestions on how to remove most of the spider webs??  (I can't stand spiders.)  I thought about pressure washing the entire interior of the garage, but that would mean taking everything out of it and I'm just not up to that.  We have a bunch of stuff up in the rafters I'd like to throw out.  Once I get that done, I'll probably put some kind of cheap (read:  more masonite) ceiling in.  Hopefully this will keep some of the critters (spiders, bugs, etc) away from me while I'm pretending that I'm working out there.<P>Whew!  I've gotten quite long winded, haven't I?  Sorry, guys.<P><P>------------------<BR>Dave-<BR>Grape Ape ('85GT)||||reg||1
Z||000012||Patrick's Dad||07-04-2000||11:32 PM||||Hey, you got the wind, we got... well... sails? (That's the best I could do, what with the Tall Ships coming in next week....)<P>I like my old house. My big thing right now is ants. Gotta get rid of them. Spiders don't bug me so much, not just because they aren't bugs, but that they do eat bugs. Just keep 'em off the Fiero. I imagine that, once you invade thier space regularly, their food supply may dry up a bit, plus that extra bit of light, might get them to move on to greener pastures. For now, cleaning is cleaning. It's all ugly.<P>Here's The Happy Brown House:<P>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/THBH.jpg">  <P>Taken with my 4X5 the weekend we moved in!<p>[This message has been edited by Patrick's Dad (edited 07-04-2000).]||||reg||10
